# The 8 MB of octal PSRAM an S3R8 carries in package, layered on top of
# sdkconfig.defaults. Leaving it out is the S3FN8 configuration, which
# is what a Cardputer would give us.
#
# STATUS: this does not boot on the DevKitC-1 N16R8 here, and the fault
# is in the hardware path rather than in anything above it.
#
# The chip's PSRAM answers on its configuration registers - vendor id
# 0x0d, 64 Mbit, good-die pass - and the MSPI timing tuning completes
# and picks a point. The first real data access then stalls the bus:
# the boot hangs inside esp_psram_extram_test(), which cpu_start runs
# right after starting the second core, and the RTC watchdog resets the
# board. It never reaches the log line that reports a failed test.
#
# Not ours, and not the speed:
#   - IDF's own hello_world example, built with these options and grown
#     to a comparable image size, hangs at the same instruction
#   - 40 MHz behaves exactly like 80 MHz
#   - single-core mode moves the hang but does not remove it
#   - with CONFIG_SPIRAM_MEMTEST=n the boot gets as far as heap_init and
#     then stalls where the pool is added to the allocator, which is the
#     next real access
#
# So the next thing to rule out is power: octal PSRAM at speed draws
# more than the ID reads do. A different supply before suspecting the
# module. Everything the port needs works without this file.
CONFIG_SPIRAM=y
CONFIG_SPIRAM_MODE_OCT=y
CONFIG_SPIRAM_SPEED_80M=y
CONFIG_SPIRAM_USE_MALLOC=y

# Left at the IDF default deliberately. Zero lets the interpreter's
# small allocations reach PSRAM, which is the only way it would benefit
# from having any, but it also lets allocations that must stay internal
# out into external memory.
CONFIG_SPIRAM_MALLOC_ALWAYSINTERNAL=16384
